{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 asserts the finite bound W(C)=c_3+c_{n-1} but provides no proof that checking amounts only up to this bound suffices. Requirement 2 explicitly demands: 'Prove it correct — in particular, justify why testing a finite, bounded set of amounts suffices, since the definition quantifies over infinitely many amounts.' The target states the bound as a bare claim with zero derivation or justification. Additionally, requirement 3 asks to 'State its worst-case time complexity as a function of n, the NUMBER OF COINS'; SRC_001 never states any explicit complexity formula (e.g., O(n·(c_3+c_{n-1}))), and does not definitively say whether a bound polynomial in n alone is achievable, only hedging with 'if a polynomial-in-n decision procedure exists (e.g. Pearson's).","target":"00f8ee2c39f6c25cf9e95c5f6090ff03f69820d5b7b2ba075a534f0d63c4bbb1"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 does not state the algorithm precisely enough to implement, violating requirement 1. The entire algorithm description is 'Pearson's algorithm checks counterexamples of the form w = k * c_i + r' — this gives no specification of what k, i, and r range over, how the test for each candidate is performed, or how the result is aggregated into a canonicity decision. Furthermore, the target conflates Pearson's algorithm (which has its own candidate-generation method independent of the Kozen-Zaks bound) with the Kozen-Zaks finite bound, producing an incoherent mechanism. No proof of correctness or of the finite bound is provided, failing requirement 2.","target":"3cfb0351575f05f8de20d3bd3d46ac930ced0b02daf84bcacd9a451a7c9e1d86"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_003's search range begins at c_2+1, which systematically misses counterexamples smaller than c_2. When the subsystem (c_3,…,c_n) is itself non-canonical, the full system's greedy representation for amounts below c_2 is identical to greedy on that subsystem (since c_1 and c_2 are too large), so a counterexample below c_2 exists. Concrete counterexample to the procedure: C=(100,10,4,3,1) is strictly decreasing with c_n=1. The smallest counterexample is w=6: greedy gives 4+1+1=3 coins while optimal is 3+3=2 coins. But SRC_003 only searches [c_2+1, c_1+c_2-1]=[11,109], never checking w=6, so it returns a false 'canonical' verdict. The upper bound c_1+c_2-1 is valid (weaker than the true Kozen-Zaks bound c_3+c_{n-1}), but the lower bound c_2+1 is an unsound omission.","counterexample":[{"amount":6,"coins":[100,10,4,3,1],"greedy_coins":3,"greedy_repr":"4+1+1","optimal_coins":2,"optimal_repr":"3+3"}],"target":"7d65ec7384dc64087c0337c23d2442bb2786f1c93c732e43db436e1dd2e2d224"}]}